A||||15||GWMORGAN||CORRODED BATTERY SUPPORT||||
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||09-10-1999||10:31 AM||||My battery tray and support is so corroded the battery is about to fall thru. Is there any way I can have it repaired/replaced. A suggestion will be appreciated.||||reg||
Z||000001||chris6878||09-10-1999||10:50 AM||||Hey...<P>The 85 GT i just purchased has the same problems... My tray is actually rusted away. They owners before me constructed the bottom of the tray with small pieces of wood. I plan on repairing this better later. My dad has a set of torches that could easily correct the problem. I was thinking of welding a piece of metal back in place of the old metal that rusted away...<P>later <BR>chris||||reg||
Z||000002||ggarsk||09-10-1999||11:13 AM||||My tray is in the early stages of rust. I wire brushed it off and used JB Weld on the bad spots. I then had a tray made out off stainless to put it there.||||reg||
Z||000003||Dale||09-10-1999||01:36 PM||||Sounds to me like its time to move the battery to the front.  Its a better place for it anyway.||||reg||
Z||000004||theogre||09-10-1999||02:14 PM||||check Kick Hill and Fiero Store. One or both have good used trays.||||reg||
Z||000005||alpha||09-10-1999||02:39 PM||||I would first get a good new tray from the Fiero Store. <P>Then I would remove the old tray. This is a pain in the neck. It is spot welded in many spots. Your best bet is to peal and pry these apart.<P>For installation you can weld, but I have heard many people have had a bad time with that. Ed Parks has suggested in the past that you should use short sheet metal screws. When I did my install, I used a combination of steel rivits (where I could) and self-drilling sheet metal screws with good restults.<P>I hope this helps.<P>--Doug Knight||||reg||
Z||000006||GREENDANO||09-10-1999||09:56 PM||||Has anyone checked the dealer?  I bought mine a year ago at the dealer I work at.  It's a replaceable part that was still available.  I don't have a part number because it's on the car.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||5
Z||000007||Mark||09-11-1999||12:04 AM||||I was able to buy a new tray and support from a local Pontiac dealer (in Pittsburgh) for about $25.  I removed the remains of the old tray using a Dremmel tool with cutoff wheel and installed the new tray with large pop rivets.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||09-11-1999||08:16 AM||||My tray is in the early stages of rusting out also. I sprayed it dowm with three coats of Extend rust killer, then cut a piece of lead to fit exactly in the tray then put the battery back in. Changed the battery terminals while I had it out also, they were getting rusted as well. Then I cut two pieces of plywood to fit perfectly over the two grills to prevent more water from getting in engine compartment. They fit nicely in trunk when I'm driving!<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 09-11-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||09-11-1999||07:37 PM||||Iam with Dale, MOVE IT!!<P>But jeg`s has a stainless steel tray and hold down, $27.99 tray, $9.99 hold down, that`s not to bad. They also have battery box kits and mounts.||||reg||5
Z||000010||GWMORGAN||09-12-1999||02:40 AM||||Any suggestion on the best location and easiest way to install the battery up front?<BR>         ANYONE...||||reg||5
Z||000011||mwbackus||09-12-1999||04:30 AM||||Both Jegs and Summit have kits for relocating the battery to the trunk of mid-engine challenged car. They work fine for moving it to the front of a Fiero, cost $40 to $120 depending on the quality of the kit.  In addition to this, I would suggest running the wires in some flex electrical conduit.  The proximity of the wires to the fuel tank is a concern, and the conduit would at least add some to the safety to this alteration.<P>Mike||||reg||3
Z||000012||GREENDANO||09-12-1999||10:07 AM||||Go here-&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Upgrades/Upgrades.html<BR>It has a battery box that goes under the spare tire.<BR>Dan-O||||reg||3
Z||000013||batboy||09-12-1999||12:01 PM||||Gokart, if I read your post correctly, you said you've blocked off those louvered rear deck grills?!? I'm not so sure I'd do that. A lot of heat is pulled out of those grills (which is what they were designed for). Water dripping down from the grills is not the main problem causing the battery tray to rust. It's the acids being vented out of the battery is the big problem. Besides, when driving on wet roads, moisture is swirled up through the engine compartment anyway. Repair or replace the battery tray (see URL below), make sure it's painted real well, and pull the battery out once in a while to flush all the acid off with running water from a hose.<P> <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/electric.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/electric.htm</A> <P>Dan-o mentioned the West Coast Fiero battery relocating box that Chris West makes. It looks like a great kit (maybe a little expensive). It mounts the battery under the spare tire in the front. But, you might hold off buying one until I get my order resolved.<P>I ordered one of those battery relocating boxes a while back and am still waiting for it. I sent a check on 7-31-99 and the check cleared on 8-12-99. I finally contacted Chris on 8-29-99 to inquire about the order. He claimed that he didn't recall ever getting the check. After a visit to my Credit Union and a couple more emails to Chris, we got it all straightened out (I think) and he said that he would ship the kit right out and that he was sorry. That was on 9-7-99 and I'm still waiting for the package. I'm hoping that it's because of busy summertime schedules and that Chris was waiting for my check to clear and just spaced my order out. I'm trying to give him the benefit of the doubt and I don't believe he was trying to scam me, but after a month and a half, I'm getting tired of waiting. Maybe a money order sent by certified mail would have been a better way to order it. I'll let you know how it turns out.||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||09-12-1999||08:31 PM||||Never mind me, Gokart! I reread your post and I now realize you just put the covers over the grills when it's parked and put them in the trunk when you're driving. Makes sense.||||reg||1
Z||000015||bHooper||09-14-1999||11:07 AM||||batboy, I went to the link but couldn't get it to open in netscape....check it and let us know for sure.<P>My battery support and mount are completely rusted away, so much so that it has even rusted a two fisted hole through the engine compartment.  On the brite side, more air to cool the engine <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  I have thought about relocating it the battery to the front, should help the ballance a little more, but first I have to cut out all of the rust from the old box. ||||reg||6
